      "caption": "\\oint\\mathbf D\\cdot d\\mathbf A=Q,\\qquad D(2\\pi r\\ell)=Q",
      "seconds": 14.0,
      "overrides": {},
      "bridge": "Using the dielectric region, choose a coaxial Gaussian surface",
      "narration": "We redraw the Gaussian cylinder coaxially inside the dielectric. Cylindrical symmetry makes the displacement field radial and constant on the curved surface, so Gauss's law gives D times 2 pi r ell equal to Q.",
      "code": "",
      "origin": "agent",
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "title": "Field in dielectric",
      "component": "equation_chain",
      "parameters": {
        "steps": [
          "D(2\\pi r\\ell)=Q",
          "\\mathbf E(r)=\\frac{Q}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\kappa\\ell r}\\,\\hat{\\mathbf r},\\quad \\kappa=5"
        ],
        "region": "stage"
      },
      "caption": "\\mathbf E(r)=\\frac{Q}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\kappa\\ell r}\\,\\hat{\\mathbf r}",
      "seconds": 17.0,
      "overrides": {},
      "bridge": "Gauss's law gives the radial field.",
      "narration": "Gauss's law gives D=Q/(2\\pi r\\ell). Using \\mathbf D=\\varepsilon_0\\kappa\\mathbf E introduces the factor 1/(\\varepsilon_0\\kappa) in the electric field.",
      "code": "",
      "origin": "agent",
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "title": "Normal component",
      "component": "equation_chain",
      "parameters": {
        "steps": [
          "r=\\sqrt{R^2+y^2}",
          "E_x=\\frac{Q}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\kappa\\ell}\\frac{R}{R^2+y^2}"
        ],
        "region": "stage"
      },
      "caption": "E_x=\\frac{QR}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\kappa\\ell(R^2+y^2)}",
      "seconds": 11.0,
      "overrides": {},
      "bridge": "The plane needs the normal component, not the full field.",
      "narration": "Flux uses the component normal to the plane, not the full radial field. At (R,y), the radial unit vector has x-component R/r, producing the stated E_x.",
      "code": "",
      "origin": "agent",
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "title": "Both segments",
      "component": "equation_chain",
      "parameters": {
        "steps": [
          "dA=\\ell\\,dy",
          "\\Phi_E=2\\ell\\int_R^{\\sqrt{3}R}\\frac{Q}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\kappa\\ell}\\frac{R}{R^2+y^2}\\,dy"
        ],
        "region": "stage"
      },
      "caption": "\\Phi_E=2\\ell\\int_R^{\\sqrt{3}R}\\frac{Q R}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\kappa\\ell(R^2+y^2)}\\,dy",
      "seconds": 13.0,
      "overrides": {},
      "bridge": "Add the equal upper and lower contributions.",
      "narration": "A strip has area dA=\\ell\\,dy. The upper and lower dielectric segments contribute equally because E_x depends on y^2, so one integral is multiplied by two.",
      "code": "",
      "origin": "agent",
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "title": "Angular integral",
      "component": "equation_chain",
      "parameters": {
        "steps": [
          "\\int_R^{\\sqrt{3}R}\\frac{R}{R^2+y^2}\\,dy",
          "=\\frac{\\pi}{3}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}=\\frac{\\pi}{12}"
        ],
        "region": "stage"
      },
      "caption": "\\int_R^{\\sqrt{3}R}\\frac{R\\,dy}{R^2+y^2}=\\frac{\\pi}{12}",
      "seconds": 12.0,
      "overrides": {},
      "bridge": "Evaluate the remaining geometric integral.",
      "narration": "The integral is an angular change in disguise. Its endpoint angles are \\pi/3 and \\pi/4, so the angular difference is \\pi/12.",
      "code": "",
      "origin": "agent",
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "title": "Simplify flux",
      "component": "equation_chain",
      "parameters": {
        "steps": [
          "\\Phi_E=2\\ell\\left(\\frac{Q}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\kappa\\ell}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{12}\\right)",
          "\\Phi_E=\\frac{Q}{12\\varepsilon_0\\kappa}"
        ],
        "region": "stage"
      },
      "caption": "\\Phi_E=\\frac{Q}{12\\varepsilon_0\\kappa}",
      "seconds": 11.0,
      "overrides": {},
      "bridge": "Substitute the angular difference.",
      "narration": "Substituting the angular integral cancels the cylinder length and the factor of \\pi. The general electric-field flux is Q/(12\\varepsilon_0\\kappa).",
      "code": "",
      "origin": "agent",
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "s10",
      "title": "Insert kappa",
      "component": "equation_chain",
      "parameters": {
        "steps": [
          "\\Phi_E=\\frac{Q}{12\\varepsilon_0\\kappa}",
          "\\kappa=5\\Rightarrow\\Phi_E=\\frac{Q}{60\\varepsilon_0}"
        ],
        "region": "stage"
      },
      "caption": "\\kappa=5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\Phi_E=\\frac{Q}{60\\varepsilon_0}",
      "seconds": 9.0,
      "overrides": {},
      "bridge": "Finally insert the dielectric constant.",
      "narration": "For this dielectric, \\kappa=5. With Q positive and the normal directed toward increasing x, the flux is positive.",
      "code": "",
      "origin": "agent",
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "s11",
      "title": "Flux and units",
      "component": "comparison",
      "parameters": {
        "left_title": "Electric-field flux",
        "right_title": "Displacement flux",
        "rows": [
